---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatStrip from '../components/khensu/StatStrip.astro';


// Drop physics, from first principles (see the table below for the inputs).
const g = 9.81;
const h = 1.5;
const v = Math.sqrt(2 * g * h);
const phone = 0.2;
const shell = 0.095;
const rigid = 0.1e-3;
const stroke = 0.3e-3;
const decel = (d: number) => (v * v) / (2 * d) / g;
const fmt = (n: number, digits = 0) => n.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: digits, minimumFractionDigits: digits });
---

<Base title="suñůlink.shield" description="Specifications for suñůlink.shield for iPhone 18 Pro: a ceramic-coated titanium node that finishes level with the lenses, paired to your iPhone's Secure Enclave.">
  <p class="eyebrow">suñůlink.shield</p>
  <h1>Your phone becomes part of the network</h1>
  <StatStrip
    items={[
      { n: 0, unit: 'mm', label: 'Above the lenses. The phone gets no thicker' },
      { n: 6, unit: 'h', label: 'On its own battery, relaying' },
      { n: 15, unit: 'min', label: 'To 50% over USB-C. Full in about 45' },
      { n: 7.5, dec: 1, unit: 'Wh', label: 'Battery capacity' },
      { n: '95 / 136', unit: 'g', label: 'Titanium and brass' },
    ]}
  />
  <p class="lede">For iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max, in ceramic-coated titanium or solid brass.</p>
  <p>It turns the phone into a node with its own battery and leaves the iPhone’s own USB-C port open.</p>

  <h2>Specifications</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Specification</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fits</td><td>iPhone 18 Pro and iPhone 18 Pro Max</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Thickness</td><td>4.7 mm on the back, level with the lens tops: +0 mm at the iPhone’s thickest point. A flat back, with only the lenses showing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Footprint</td><td>71.0 × 105.9 mm, covering the back below the camera plateau, inset 0.4 mm from the phone’s outline and following its edge curve, so it never overhangs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shell</td><td>CNC-machined Grade 5 titanium (Ti-6Al-4V), with a ceramic finish: a plasma electrolytic oxide layer grown from the titanium itself, sealed with a colour-matched ceramic topcoat in Burgundy, Glacier, Silver or Black. Or bare solid brass (C26000), which darkens to a natural patina.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weight</td><td>95 g in titanium, 136 g in brass</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Battery</td><td>7.5 Wh (about 1,950 mAh at 3.85 V), about 6 hours as a node on its own; 50% in 15 minutes, full in about 45</td></tr>
      <tr><td>USB-C</td><td>The shell has its own USB-C port in its bottom edge, above the iPhone’s. The iPhone’s port stays open: charge both at once, from two cables or one dual USB-C cable, and keep using the iPhone’s port for drives, displays, audio and CarPlay.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Attachment</td><td>MagSafe-compatible magnet ring and a 0.5 mm micro-suction urethane layer that is also the shock isolator</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Antennas</td><td>A band in the bottom edge, so nearby nodes reach each other directly where there’s no internet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mesh range</td><td>Without the shield, phones reach each other over Bluetooth and Wi-Fi peer-to-peer: about 30 m indoors, 100 m outdoors. With it, over Wi-Fi HaLow: about 600 m shield to shield on open ground (100 m in a city), and 2 km to a rooftop node in the open (250 m in a city, 500 m in suburbs). Every hop adds the same again.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Camera</td><td>Clears every lens, flash and sensor, outside each one’s field of view</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="footnote">
    Figures for iPhone 18 Pro. Mesh ranges are calculated from the link budget: HaLow at +21 dBm and −105 dBm sensitivity (1 MHz, MCS10), a −3 dBi antenna in the shell, a +3 dBi antenna 10 m up on the rooftop node and a 15 dB margin for the body and fading, with the two-ray ground model in the open and Okumura–Hata in towns, in the US 902–928 MHz band. Europe’s lower power limit makes them about a third shorter. Battery life and charge time vary with use and settings. iPhone, MagSafe and CarPlay are trademarks of Apple Inc.
  </p>

  <h2>Inside</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Hardware</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Node computer</td><td><a href="https://www.nxp.com/products/i.MX93">NXP i.MX 93</a> (2× Arm Cortex-A55, Cortex-M33, EdgeLock Secure Enclave), 2 GB LPDDR4X, 32 GB eMMC</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Network guard</td><td><a href="https://www.st.com/en/microcontrollers-microprocessors/stm32u5-series.html">STMicroelectronics STM32U5</a> (Arm Cortex-M33 with TrustZone), running open Rust firmware with post-quantum secure boot. It owns every radio.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mesh radio</td><td>Wi-Fi HaLow (802.11ah), <a href="https://www.morsemicro.com/products/chips/mm6108">Morse Micro MM6108</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wi-Fi and Bluetooth</td><td><a href="https://www.nxp.com/products/IW612">NXP IW612</a>: Wi-Fi 6, Bluetooth LE, 802.15.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Battery</td><td>Single-cell lithium-ion pouch, 3.85 V, about 1,950 mAh (7.5 Wh), shaped to the shell, with protection and temperature sensing. Certified to IEC 62133-2 and UN 38.3.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>USB-C Power Delivery</td><td><a href="https://www.ti.com/product/TPS25750">Texas Instruments TPS25750</a> controller</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Charger</td><td><a href="https://www.ti.com/product/BQ25895">Texas Instruments BQ25895</a>, single cell, with power path</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fuel gauge</td><td><a href="https://www.ti.com/product/BQ27427">Texas Instruments BQ27427</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shock layer</td><td>Microcellular urethane, <a href="https://www.rogerscorp.com/elastomeric-material-solutions">Rogers PORON XRD</a> class, 0.5 mm, with a micro-suction face</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Board</td><td>34 × 14 × 1.6 mm</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="footnote">
    NXP, i.MX, EdgeLock, STMicroelectronics, STM32, Morse
    Micro, Texas Instruments, Rogers, PORON, Arm, Cortex, TrustZone, Wi-Fi HaLow and Bluetooth are trademarks of their
    respective owners.
  </p>

  <h2 id="strength">Stronger than the phone</h2>
  <p>
    iPhone 18 Pro is an aluminium unibody with a Ceramic Shield back. The shell is Grade 5 titanium, which is stronger
    and harder than any aluminium alloy used in phones, so on the back it is a shield, not just a cover.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Yield strength</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Grade 5 titanium, Ti-6Al-4V (the shell)</td><td>at least 828 MPa (ASTM B348)</td><td>About 1.6× high-strength aluminium</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7075-T6 aluminium</td><td>about 500 MPa</td><td>The strongest common 7000-series alloy. Apple doesn’t publish the iPhone 18 Pro alloy.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>C26000 brass, half hard (the brass shell)</td><td>about 360 MPa</td><td>Softer than titanium; chosen for its antimicrobial surface</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="shock">Drops: the physics</h2>
  <p>
    A phone dropped from {h} m hits the ground at √(2gh) ≈ {fmt(v, 1)} m/s. What breaks glass is how fast that speed
    goes to zero: stopping over a distance d means an average deceleration of v²/2d. A hard metal shell pressed
    straight onto the phone would pass its own {shell * 1000} g into the phone in a rigid contact that stops within about
    0.1 mm, around {fmt(decel(rigid))} g.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shell is held on by a 0.5 mm microcellular urethane layer instead. In a back or edge impact the shell stops
    first and the layer lets the phone decelerate over about 0.3 mm of usable stroke, around {fmt(decel(stroke))} g on
    average: about a third of the rigid case. The layer firms up at impact speeds and returns to shape after each hit.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Drop height</td><td>{h} m, the usual test height</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Impact speed</td><td>{fmt(v, 2)} m/s</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Phone mass</td><td>about {phone * 1000} g</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Energy at impact</td><td>{fmt(0.5 * phone * v * v, 1)} J for the phone alone, {fmt(0.5 * (phone + shell) * v * v, 1)} J with the shell</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rigid stop</td><td>about 0.1 mm: {fmt(decel(rigid))} g average</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Through the shock layer</td><td>about 0.3 mm of 0.5 mm: {fmt(decel(stroke))} g average</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The shell covers the back and frames the camera. It doesn’t cover the screen, so a drop flat onto the glass is still
    the iPhone’s Ceramic Shield 2 against the ground. Every shell is tested to MIL-STD-810H Method 516.8 drops at 1.2 m and 1.5 m onto steel-backed plywood.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pairing">Paired to your iPhone’s Secure Enclave</h2>
  <p>
    At setup, the khėnsu.ñü app creates a post-quantum ML-DSA-87 key inside your iPhone’s Secure Enclave
    (<a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/cryptokit/enhancing-your-app-s-privacy-and-security-with-quantum-secure-workflows">CryptoKit, iOS 26</a>),
    usable only with Face ID, and signs the shell’s own identity key with it. The shell keeps its keys sealed in the
    i.MX 93’s EdgeLock Secure Enclave and takes instructions only from the paired key. It carries only sealed,
    fixed-size packets on a constant schedule, so it never holds a readable message or learns who is talking to whom.
    If you lose it, revoke it from your iPhone; there’s nothing on it for the finder to read.
  </p>
  <p>
    No device can honestly be called unhackable. The guarantee is narrower and testable: no single compromised part,
    whether the radio, the node computer or the network, can reveal content, metadata or your identity. The guard
    chip owns every radio, so even a compromised node computer can’t break the cover pattern. Firmware is open and
    reproducibly built.
  </p>

  <h2 id="design">Design documents</h2>
